  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nCurrently, mostly clear skies across western and central North\nDakota. A weak, dry cold front has pushed through all but the\nfar southeast portion of the state. Temperatures behind the\nfront were in the lower 30s to mid 40s. A few areas of patchy\nfog were noted where temperature/dew point spread were close.\n\nFor today, high pressure slides east across the state. Generally\nlight winds from the north will shift east to southeast through\nthe day. Western ND will see some stronger southeast winds \n(around 15-20 mph) mid to late afternoon. Highs today will be \njust a bit cooler than Tuesday for most areas, but still well \nabove normal. Lows tonight will range from the mid 30s to mid \n40s. \n\nThursday and Friday will see the warmest temperatures of the\nweek. Highs each day will range from the mid 70s to lower 80s,\nwith warmest temperatures each day in the southwest. We \nutilized a blend of NBM and NBM90 for today through Friday. \nToday was a 50/50 blend with Thursday and Friday each leaning \ntowards the warmer NBM90 temperatures. Near record, to record\nhighs will be possible, especially southwest and south central.\n\nAn upper level trough lifts into the Region Friday into\nSaturday. This will bring an increasing southerly surface flow\nto western and central ND Friday, with a strong west to\nnorthwest surface flow on Saturday.\n\nFriday afternoon and evening will see mainly low chances for\nshowers and possibly an isolated thunderstorm. Moderate to\nstrong shear lifts through the forecast area with some elevated\nCAPE, as high as around 600 J/kg over western ND around 00Z \nSaturday. With the warm temperatures expected. Low level CIN \nwill remain and will be hard to overcome. The highest \nprecipitation chances will be Friday night as wrap around \nmoisture lifts through the northwest portion of the state behind\nthe surface low that lifts northward into southern Canada. Much\ncooler air will also spread into the State on Saturday, ushered\nin by the strong west to northwest winds. After highs in the \n70s and lower 80s Friday. Highs Saturday are forecast to range \nfrom the mid 40s northwest to the upper 50s over the southern \nJames River Valley. \n\nFriday and Saturday may also produce some near critical fire\nweather conditions. Friday, southerly winds around 15 to 25 mph\ndevelop over southwest ND, with temperatures approaching 80\ndegrees and low humidities. The far southwest could see a few \nhours of near critical fire weather conditions. On Saturday, \nstrong winds will spread east across the state. By Saturday \nafternoon, very dry air will spread into the state. At this \ntime it looks like the dry filters into the state behind the \nstrong winds. Thus if there are some near critical conditions, \nthey would be brief. The much cooler air will also aid in \nkeeping near critical fire weather conditions at bay.\n\nCooler conditions remain in the forecast Sunday and through the\nfirst half of the workweek. There are a couple of systems that\nmay approach our local area early in the workweek but\nuncertainty remains high in timing and placement. Currently the\nNBM is advertising only some hit and miss slight chance pops \nfrom late Sunday through Tuesday.",
